REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w05","title":"Tenses and subject–verb agreement","summary":"How English shows time on the verb. Three tenses (past, present, future) and four aspects (simple, perfect, progressive, perfect progressive) make twelve forms, each with its meanings and its structure. Then subject–verb agreement: the person and number of the subject must show up on the verb, so the real work is finding the subject.","n":5,"lectures":[{"code":"5.1","title":"Past tense | simple, perfect, progressive, and perfect progressive forms","video":"6Ktk0Gio9wo","seconds":1642},{"code":"5.2","title":"Present tense | simple, perfect, progressive, and perfect progressive forms","video":"9lzN9jZiWiw","seconds":1814},{"code":"5.3","title":"Future tense | simple, perfect, progressive, and perfect progressive forms","video":"qTJ9gMjcb1c","seconds":1578},{"code":"5.4","title":"Agreement in english sentences (subject & verb in sentences)","video":"pv-eV3nVceE","seconds":2052},{"code":"5.5","title":"Practise subject verb agreement in english","video":"ALjHStRxE7M","seconds":714}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"tense-and-aspect","level":2,"title":"Tense and aspect"},{"id":"the-past-tense","level":2,"title":"The past tense"},{"id":"simple-past","level":3,"title":"Simple past"},{"id":"past-perfect","level":3,"title":"Past perfect"},{"id":"past-progressive","level":3,"title":"Past progressive"},{"id":"past-perfect-progressive","level":3,"title":"Past perfect progressive"},{"id":"the-present-tense","level":2,"title":"The present tense"},{"id":"simple-present","level":3,"title":"Simple present"},{"id":"present-perfect","level":3,"title":"Present perfect"},{"id":"present-progressive","level":3,"title":"Present progressive"},{"id":"present-perfect-progressive","level":3,"title":"Present perfect progressive"},{"id":"the-future-tense","level":2,"title":"The future tense"},{"id":"will-and-time-adverbials","level":3,"title":"Will and time adverbials"},{"id":"simple-future","level":3,"title":"Simple future"},{"id":"future-perfect","level":3,"title":"Future perfect"},{"id":"future-progressive","level":3,"title":"Future progressive"},{"id":"future-perfect-progressive","level":3,"title":"Future perfect progressive"},{"id":"the-twelve-forms-together","level":2,"title":"The twelve forms together"},{"id":"subject-verb-agreement","level":2,"title":"Subject–verb agreement"},{"id":"how-agreement-works","level":3,"title":"How agreement works"},{"id":"nouns-that-need-a-second-look","level":3,"title":"Nouns that need a second look"},{"id":"when-the-verb-agrees-with-another-noun","level":3,"title":"When the verb agrees with another noun"},{"id":"agreement-practice","level":2,"title":"Agreement practice"},{"id":"and-or-nor","level":3,"title":"And, or, nor"},{"id":"doesn-t-and-don-t","level":3,"title":"Doesn’t and don’t"},{"id":"a-phrase-between-subject-and-verb","level":3,"title":"A phrase between subject and verb"},{"id":"singular-words-and-plural-words","level":3,"title":"Singular words and plural words"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tense</strong> is the verb form that places an action in <strong>time</strong>: past, present or future. <strong>Aspect</strong> adds detail about how the action sits in that time: <strong>simple</strong> (plain, absolute time), <strong>perfect</strong> (one time <strong>embedded</strong> in, that is before, another: relational time), <strong>progressive</strong> (the action is <strong>in progress</strong>), <strong>perfect progressive</strong> (an embedded action in progress). Three tenses × four aspects = twelve forms.</li>\n<li>In every form, <strong>one</strong> part carries the tense: the <em>-ed</em> ending (or a changed verb, <em>drove</em>) in the simple past, or else the <strong>first auxiliary</strong>: <em>did, had, was, were</em> (past); <em>do, does, has, have, am, is, are</em> (present); <em>will</em> (future). The <strong>V3</strong> (past participle: <em>returned, given</em>) and the <em><strong>-ing</strong></em> form never carry tense. The simple present affirmative has <strong>no visible tense marker</strong>: the <em>-s</em> of <em>drinks</em> is an <strong>agreement</strong> marker.</li>\n<li><strong>Past</strong> (5.1): <em>She returned</em> (past and finished; <em>did</em> + bare verb in negatives and questions) · <em>She had returned … before the semester began</em> (past before past) · <em>She was walking</em> (in progress then) · <em>She had been writing … before she published</em> (an earlier past action in progress).</li>\n<li><strong>Present</strong> (5.2): <em>He reads the newspaper in the morning</em> (a state, a habit, an action now) · <em>The train has finally arrived</em> (past with relevance now; <em>for, since, just, already, yet</em>) · <em>She is eating a mango</em> (in progress now) · <em>The baby has been crying for a long time</em> (began in the past, continues now).</li>\n<li><strong>Future</strong> (5.3): <em><strong>will</strong></em> (a modal auxiliary that suggests prediction) + the bare verb, often with a time adverbial (<em>in March, in the morning</em>) · <em>She will have completed her work by then</em> (past within the future) · <em>She will be taking the train</em> (timetabled future, routine, habit) · <em>I will have been living in Chennai for over ten years</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement</strong> (5.4): the <strong>person</strong> and <strong>number</strong> of the subject must show up on the verb; gender takes no part in English. <em>Raju likes pizza</em>, <em>Raju and Ramu like pizza</em>. <em>I</em> and <em>you</em> follow the plural pattern (<em>I go</em>, <em>you go</em>, <em>I don’t</em>), and <em>am</em> belongs to <em>I</em> alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the real subject</strong> (5.4, 5.5): <em>and</em> → plural; <em>or / nor</em> → the noun nearer the verb; a phrase in between changes nothing (<em>one of the boxes <strong>is</strong></em>). Singular: <em>everyone, each, either</em>; <em>mathematics, news</em>; an amount (<em>five years, five dollars</em>); collective nouns (<em>team, family</em>). Plural: <em>glasses, jeans, police, scissors, trousers</em>. <em>Sheep</em> and <em>deer</em> show their number only on the verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"tense-and-aspect\">Tense and aspect</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every language needs a way to say <strong>when</strong> something happens. English does it mainly through the <strong>verb</strong>. We change the verb (<em>return → returned</em>), put a small helping word in front of it (<em>will return</em>), or both (<em>had returned</em>). These verb forms are the <strong>tense</strong> forms. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a></p>\n<p>There are two ways to place an action in time. <em>She returned from the conference yesterday</em> gives a time of its own: yesterday, in the past. This is <strong>absolute</strong> time. <em>She had returned from the conference before the semester began</em> places one past time <strong>in relation to</strong> another: the return came before the beginning. This is <strong>relational</strong> time. Tense alone cannot say this; for it English adds a second tool, the <strong>aspect</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tense</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Tense</strong> is “a grammatical category or a grammatical form in the English language that is primarily used to express time”. To show time, English makes changes <strong>around the verb</strong>, the most important element of the sentence: a suffix on the verb (<em>-ed</em>), a change inside the verb (<em>drive → drove</em>), or an extra verb in front of it, an <strong>auxiliary</strong> (helping) verb (<em>did, had, will</em>). There are three tenses: <strong>past, present, future</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Aspect</strong> tells <strong>how</strong> the action is carried out in a period of time. It is what lets us talk about <strong>relational</strong> time. Aspect forms <strong>combine with</strong> the tense forms. They are shown by an extra particle (such as <em>have</em> or <em>be</em>) or by a change to the verb (such as <em>-ing</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>What it adds</th>\n<th>How it is made</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>simple</td>\n<td>nothing: plain, absolute time</td>\n<td>no extra marker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perfect</td>\n<td>one time embedded in (before) another</td>\n<td>a form of <em>have</em> + V3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>progressive</td>\n<td>the action is in progress</td>\n<td>a form of <em>be</em> + verb-<em>ing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>perfect progressive</td>\n<td>an embedded action in progress</td>\n<td>a form of <em>have</em> + <em>been</em> + verb-<em>ing</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<p>Three tenses with four aspects give <strong>twelve</strong> forms. Lecture 5.1 covers the past, 5.2 the present, 5.3 the future.</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Words this page uses</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>V1</strong> is the base (bare) form, as in the dictionary: <em>return, eat, go</em>. <strong>V2</strong> is the simple past form: <em>returned, ate, went</em>. <strong>V3</strong> is the <strong>past participle</strong>: <em>returned, eaten, gone</em>. The <strong>-ing form</strong>: <em>returning, eating, going</em>. A <strong>participle</strong> (V3 or the <em>-ing</em> form) cannot show tense by itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Regular</strong> verbs make V2 and V3 with <em>-ed</em>. <strong>Irregular</strong> verbs change inside the word: <em>eat, ate, eaten</em>; <em>buy, bought, bought</em>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>auxiliary</strong> (helping) verb helps the <strong>main verb</strong>: <em>be</em> (<em>am, is, are, was, were, been</em>), <em>do</em> (<em>do, does, did</em>), <em>have</em> (<em>have, has, had</em>), and the modal <em>will</em>. The main verb carries the meaning: <em>finish, eat, write</em>. (Auxiliaries were introduced in <a href=\"#/english1/w02/verbs\">Week 2</a>.)</li>\n<li>The <strong>tense marker</strong> is the part that shows the tense. An <strong>agreement marker</strong> shows the subject’s person and number (the <em>-s</em> of <em>she drinks</em>). An <strong>aspect marker</strong> shows the aspect (<em>-ing</em> for the progressive).</li>\n<li>An <strong>affirmative</strong> sentence is a positive statement. A <strong>negative</strong> sentence has <em>not</em>, the <strong>negation marker</strong>. An <strong>interrogative</strong> sentence is a question: a <strong>yes/no question</strong> (<em>Did you go to college today?</em>) or a <strong>wh-question</strong>, which starts with <em>what, where, when …</em> and asks for content (<em>What did he eat?</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Person</strong>: first person = the speaker (<em>I, we</em>); second person = the listener (<em>you</em>); third person = anyone or anything else (<em>he, she, it, they, Raju, the school</em>). <strong>Number</strong>: <strong>singular</strong> (one) or <strong>plural</strong> (more than one).</li>\n<li>✗ marks a wrong sentence.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The verbs of this week’s lectures, in their three forms:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>V1 (base)</th>\n<th>V2 (simple past)</th>\n<th>V3 (past participle)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>return</td>\n<td>returned</td>\n<td>returned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>reach</td>\n<td>reached</td>\n<td>reached</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>drive</td>\n<td>drove</td>\n<td>driven</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>steal</td>\n<td>stole</td>\n<td>stolen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>give</td>\n<td>gave</td>\n<td>given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>go</td>\n<td>went</td>\n<td>gone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>come</td>\n<td>came</td>\n<td>come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>eat</td>\n<td>ate</td>\n<td>eaten</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>buy</td>\n<td>bought</td>\n<td>bought</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>be</td>\n<td>was / were</td>\n<td>been</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lectures name these verbs and use the forms they need (<em>drive, drove</em>; <em>steal, stole</em>; <em>eat, ate, eaten</em>; <em>buy, bought, bought</em>; <em>had given</em>; <em>have gone</em>; <em>has come</em>; <em>had been</em>). The other cells are the standard forms, added here so that each row is complete.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-past-tense\">The past tense</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 5.1 gives four ways to talk about past time. Each one answers a different question about a past action:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did it happen, and is it finished? <strong>Simple past</strong>: <em>She returned from the conference yesterday.</em></li>\n<li>Did it happen before another past event? <strong>Past perfect</strong>: <em>She had returned from the conference before the semester began.</em></li>\n<li>Was it going on at that time? <strong>Past progressive</strong>: <em>She was walking back to her lab.</em></li>\n<li>Had it been going on before another past event? <strong>Past perfect progressive</strong>: <em>She had been writing a book on climate change before she published her recent work.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"simple-past\">Simple past</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple past</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The simple past “is the most commonly used tense to refer to past time”. It signifies two points about the time of the action: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li>the event is <strong>in the past</strong>;</li>\n<li>the event is <strong>finished</strong> (completed).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentence: <em>She <strong>returned</strong> from the conference yesterday.</em> The suffix <em>-ed</em> on <em>return</em> tells us that the sentence is in the simple past. Here <em>-ed</em> is the <strong>tense marker</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Regular and irregular verbs in the simple past</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Regular</strong> verbs add the suffix <em><strong>-ed</strong></em> to the main verb: <em>return → returned</em>, <em>arrange → arranged</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Irregular</strong> verbs take no <em>-ed</em>. The word changes <strong>inside</strong> instead, usually one of its sounds: <em>drive → drove</em>, <em>steal → stole</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The simple past is also used to describe a character, a state of mind or an event in the past. The slide: “The use of simple past tense is also common in <strong>fictional narration</strong> to describe events and states as imagined in the past.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The simple past in a story (the lecture’s excerpt at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>One evening after dark a young man <strong>prowled</strong> among these crumbling red mansions, ringing their bells. At the twelfth he <strong>rested</strong> his lean hand baggage upon the step and <strong>wiped</strong> the dust from his hatband and forehead. The bell <strong>sounded</strong> faint and far away in some remote, hollow depths.</em> (O. Henry, “The Furnished Room”)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Underline the verbs in the past tense: <em>prowled, rested, wiped, sounded</em>. Each one ends in <em>-ed</em>.</li>\n<li>Each verb is an event that the writer imagines in the past.</li>\n<li>The events come one after another and move the story forward.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the simple past (<em>prowled, rested, wiped, sounded</em>) narrates the events and states of the story as happening in the past.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Other tenses in a story</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Fiction may use the other tense forms too; there is no restriction. But the meaning they produce will be different from that of a past-tense narration. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The structure, from the slide. For tense, always look at the <strong>verb</strong>: that is where the changes happen. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence type</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>What carries the past</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Affirmative</td>\n<td><em>She <strong>arranged</strong> her clothes in the wardrobe.</em></td>\n<td>the <em>-ed</em> of <em>arranged</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td><em>She <strong>did</strong> not <strong>finish</strong> her work on time.</em></td>\n<td>the auxiliary <em>did</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yes/no question</td>\n<td><em><strong>Did</strong> you <strong>go</strong> to college today?</em> <em><strong>Did</strong> you not <strong>go</strong> to college today?</em></td>\n<td><em>did</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wh-question</td>\n<td><em>What <strong>did</strong> he <strong>eat</strong>?</em></td>\n<td><em>did</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Did carries the tense</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the negative sentence and in both kinds of question, the simple past adds the auxiliary <em><strong>did</strong></em>. <em>Did</em> is the <strong>tense marker</strong>. The main verb (<em>finish, go, eat</em>) stays in its <strong>bare form</strong>, V1, with nothing added to it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n<p>Structure: <strong>did</strong> (+ <em>not</em>) + <strong>V1</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Only one past marker</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Once <em>did</em> carries the past, the main verb does not: <em>She did not <strong>finish</strong> her work</em>, not ✗ <em>She did not finished her work</em>; <em>What did he <strong>eat</strong>?</em>, not ✗ <em>What did he ate?</em> (The rule is the lecture’s; the wrong forms are shown here for contrast.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"past-perfect\">Past perfect</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Sometimes two things happened in the past, one before the other. The <strong>perfect</strong> aspect shows which came first. It does not give an absolute time; it gives a time <strong>in relation to</strong> another time. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Past perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The perfect aspect in the past signifies (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>a past state of action in relation to the <strong>previous past</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>past before past</strong>, or the <strong>embedded past</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Of two past events, the earlier one is the <strong>previous past</strong>. The later one is the <strong>current past</strong>, or simply the past action. The earlier event is <strong>embedded</strong> in the later one: the lecturer draws it as a small box inside a big box. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Which event came first? (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>She <strong>had returned</strong> from the conference before the semester <strong>began</strong>.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Find the two events (the two verbs): <em>had returned</em> and <em>began</em>.</li>\n<li>Name them. <strong>Event 1</strong>: <em>she had returned from the conference</em>. <strong>Event 2</strong>: <em>the semester began</em>.</li>\n<li>Both events are in the past.</li>\n<li>Event 1 happened before Event 2. So Event 1 is the previous past, embedded in Event 2.</li>\n<li>The past perfect (<em>had returned</em>) marks the earlier event. The later event is in the simple past (<em>began</em>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> first she returned from the conference; after that, the semester began.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer’s timeline, read from left to right (earlier to later): <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Previous past (Event 1)</th>\n<th>Current past (Event 2)</th>\n<th>Now</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>she <strong>had returned</strong> from the conference</td>\n<td>the semester <strong>began</strong></td>\n<td>the present</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the past perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>had</strong> + <strong>V3</strong> (the past participle of the main verb). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>had</strong></em> is the auxiliary that carries the past tense: it is the <strong>tense marker</strong>.</li>\n<li>The main verb is in its <strong>V3</strong> form. In <em>had return<strong>ed</strong></em>, the <em>-ed</em> is <strong>not</strong> the simple past ending: it makes the participle. A participle cannot provide tense, so <em>had</em> is needed. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence type</th>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>had + V3</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Affirmative</td>\n<td><em>She <strong>had returned</strong> from the conference.</em></td>\n<td><em>returned</em>, V3 of <em>return</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td><em>The college <strong>had</strong> not <strong>given</strong> the permission to participate in the event.</em></td>\n<td><em>given</em>, V3 of <em>give</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plural subject</td>\n<td><em>They <strong>had reached</strong> home before the parcel arrived.</em></td>\n<td><em>reached</em>, V3 of <em>reach</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Not</em> adds negation, but the structure does not change. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></li>\n<li>A plural subject (<em>they</em>) changes nothing either: <em>had</em> is the same for every subject. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></li>\n<li><em>Reach</em> is a regular verb, so its V3 ends in <em>-ed</em>. An irregular verb changes inside instead (<em>give → given</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The second event may stay unsaid</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>She had returned from the conference</em> names only one event. That is fine: <em>had</em> + V3 alone tells the listener that this action is embedded in some later past time. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">V3 after had, not V2</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>After <em>had</em> (and after <em>has, have, will have</em>), the verb is the past participle: <em>had <strong>given</strong></em>, <em>have <strong>eaten</strong></em>, not ✗ <em>had gave</em>, ✗ <em>have ate</em>. In regular verbs V2 and V3 look the same (<em>returned</em>), so the difference shows only in irregular verbs.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"past-progressive\">Past progressive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Past progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The progressive aspect in the past is used to convey that an action was <strong>ongoing</strong> in the past”: the action was in progress, continuing, at that time. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></p>\n<p>Structure: <strong>was / were</strong> + verb-<em><strong>ing</strong></em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>was</em> and <em>were</em> are the past forms of the verb <em>be</em>. They carry the <strong>tense</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <em>-ing</em> suffix is the <strong>progressive</strong> marker.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s example: <em>Kapil <strong>was reading</strong> “Love in the Time of Cholera” by Gabriel Marquez.</em> Here <em>was</em> is <em>be</em> in the past (tense), and <em>read</em> + <em>-ing</em> is the progressive. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>be in the past</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She <strong>was walking</strong> back to her lab.</em></td>\n<td>singular</td>\n<td>was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They <strong>were talking</strong> about the cultural fest to be held next month.</em></td>\n<td>plural</td>\n<td>were</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She <strong>was</strong> not <strong>living</strong> in France back then.</em></td>\n<td>singular</td>\n<td>was</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They <strong>were</strong> not <strong>living</strong> in France back then.</em></td>\n<td>plural</td>\n<td>were</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Was</em> goes with a singular subject and <em>were</em> with a plural subject. The <em>-ing</em> stays the same. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"past-perfect-progressive\">Past perfect progressive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Past perfect progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“It is formed by adding the progressive form to the past perfect.” “It conveys the meaning that a <strong>past embedded action was in progress</strong>”: the earlier of two past actions was going on for some time. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a></p>\n<p>Structure: <strong>had</strong> + <strong>been</strong> + verb-<em><strong>ing</strong></em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>had</strong></em> is the auxiliary that carries the past tense.</li>\n<li><em><strong>been</strong></em> is the V3 of <em>be</em>. <em>Had been</em> together make the <strong>perfect</strong>.</li>\n<li>verb + <em><strong>-ing</strong></em> is the <strong>progressive</strong> marker.</li>\n<li>Neither <em>been</em> (a participle) nor <em>-ing</em> (an aspect marker) can carry tense; only <em>had</em> does.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">An earlier action in progress (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>She <strong>had been writing</strong> a book on climate change before she <strong>published</strong> her recent work.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Event 1, the previous (embedded) past: <em>she had been writing a book on climate change</em>.</li>\n<li>Event 2, the later past: <em>she published her recent work</em>.</li>\n<li>Event 1 came first, and the <em>-ing</em> shows that it went on for some time: it was <strong>in progress</strong> in the previous past.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the writing was going on before the publishing. The past perfect progressive shows an embedded past action in progress.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>She <strong>had been knitting</strong> the quilt for the baby before the family arrived for Christmas.</em> (affirmative: the knitting is Event 1, the family’s arrival is Event 2)</li>\n<li><em>Maya <strong>had</strong> not <strong>been working</strong> from the office.</em> (negative: <em>not</em> changes nothing else) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a></li>\n<li><em>The students <strong>had</strong> not <strong>been responding</strong> positively to the classes.</em> (plural subject: still <em>had been</em>) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Verbs of the mind usually stay simple</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Verbs that show <strong>mental processes</strong>, such as <em>enjoy, appreciate, love, understand</em>, “more commonly use the simple aspect than the progressive”. The lecturer asks us to avoid progressive forms of these verbs, in the past and in the present. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6Ktk0Gio9wo&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a></p>\n<p>For example (ours): <em>I understand the lesson</em>, not ✗ <em>I am understanding the lesson</em>.</p>\n<p>The rule holds best for verbs of a <strong>state</strong>: <em>understand, know, believe, want</em>. <em>Enjoy</em> is often progressive in everyday standard English: <em>I am enjoying the party</em> is correct.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-present-tense\">The present tense</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 5.2 gives four ways to talk about the present:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is it true now, a habit, or happening as I speak? <strong>Simple present</strong>: <em>He reads the newspaper in the morning.</em></li>\n<li>Did something happen in the past that matters now? <strong>Present perfect</strong>: <em>The train has finally arrived.</em></li>\n<li>Is it going on right now, for a limited time? <strong>Present progressive</strong>: <em>She is eating a mango.</em></li>\n<li>Did it start in the past and go on until now? <strong>Present perfect progressive</strong>: <em>The baby has been crying for a long time.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"simple-present\">Simple present</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple present</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The simple present is the most basic form for the present time. It refers to the <strong>absolute</strong> present. The slide gives its three major meanings: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>a state that exists at the present time</strong>: <em>I <strong>want</strong> a pencil and a pen.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>a habitual action</strong>, an action repeated on many occasions: <em>He <strong>reads</strong> the newspaper in the morning.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>an action that is happening at the present time</strong>, as the speaker talks: <em>Here <strong>comes</strong> my friend, Shikha.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the simple present</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Affirmative</strong>: subject + V1. With a third person singular subject (<em>he, she, it</em>, a singular noun), the verb takes <em><strong>-s</strong></em>: <em>she drinks</em>, but <em>they drink</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Negative and questions</strong>: <em><strong>do / does</strong></em> + V1. <em>Does</em> goes with the third person singular; <em>do</em> goes with all the others (<em>I, you, we, they</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence type</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentences</th>\n<th>Tense marker</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Affirmative</td>\n<td><em>She <strong>drinks</strong> tea in the evening.</em></td>\n<td>none visible (∅)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>do</strong> not <strong>want</strong> samosas.</em> <em>She <strong>does</strong> not <strong>make</strong> flower bouquets.</em></td>\n<td><em>do</em>, <em>does</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yes/no question</td>\n<td><em><strong>Do</strong> you <strong>sing</strong> songs?</em> <em><strong>Does</strong> she not <strong>wear</strong> hats?</em></td>\n<td><em>do</em>, <em>does</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wh-question</td>\n<td><em>What <strong>does</strong> he <strong>like</strong>?</em> <em>Where <strong>do</strong> you <strong>live</strong>?</em></td>\n<td><em>does</em>, <em>do</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In the negatives and the questions, the auxiliary <em>do</em> or <em>does</em> is the tense marker, and the main verb (<em>want, make, sing, wear, like, live</em>) stays bare. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The -s of drinks is not the tense marker</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>It is tempting to think that the <em>-s</em> in <em>drinks</em> shows the present. It does not. It is an <strong>agreement marker</strong>: it shows that the verb agrees with the singular subject <em>she</em>. Make the subject plural and the <em>-s</em> disappears, yet the sentence is still present: <em>They <strong>drink</strong> tea in the evening.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n<p>So the simple present affirmative has <strong>no visible tense marker</strong>. The present tense is there, but nothing on the surface shows it. The lecturer writes this empty marker as ∅ (“zero”). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a> (Many grammar books call <em>-s</em> the “third person singular present” ending, because it appears only in the present; the lecture separates its two jobs and gives the tense part to ∅.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"present-perfect\">Present perfect</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Present perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“It refers to a present state of action <strong>in relation to the past</strong>”: an action that happened in the past and has relevance in the present. It is relational time: the present in relation to the past. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a> It can give three meanings:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The current event is also a previous event.</strong> <em>It <strong>has drizzled</strong> all day.</em> The drizzling went on before and is still the event now; the sentence does not say whether it has stopped. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>A change in the present results from a recently finished event.</strong> <em>The train <strong>has</strong> finally <strong>arrived</strong>.</em> <em>I <strong>have learned</strong> driving.</em> The learning is over, but its result is here now: I could not drive before, and I can now. (Standard English says <em>I have learned to drive</em>.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>A present state continues from a past time.</strong> <em>I <strong>have owned</strong> the scooter for some time.</em> I had the scooter then, and I still have it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the present perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>has / have</strong> + <strong>V3</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Has</em> goes with the third person singular; <em>have</em> with all other subjects (<em>have I, have you, have we, have they</em>). This auxiliary is in the present and carries the tense. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></li>\n<li>The main verb is the V3. The <em>-ed</em> of <em>arrived</em> is the past participle, not the past tense: “do not get confused because of the suffix <em>-ed</em>”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence type</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>V3</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Affirmative</td>\n<td><em>The train <strong>has</strong> finally <strong>arrived</strong>.</em></td>\n<td><em>arrived</em> (arrive)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td><em>I <strong>have</strong> not <strong>gone</strong> on a holiday for a long time.</em></td>\n<td><em>gone</em> (go)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td><em>She <strong>has</strong> not <strong>come</strong> home since December 2021.</em></td>\n<td><em>come</em> (come)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td><em><strong>Has</strong> she <strong>bought</strong> the book yet?</em></td>\n<td><em>bought</em> (buy, bought, bought)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td><em><strong>Have</strong> you <strong>eaten</strong> already?</em></td>\n<td><em>eaten</em> (eat, ate, eaten)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Come</em> and <em>buy</em> are irregular verbs: they do not take <em>-ed</em>. <em>Buy</em> forms its V3 by changing inside: <em>bought</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Time adverbials with the present perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>time adverbial</strong> is a word or phrase that works like an adverb and tells <strong>when</strong> or <strong>for how long</strong>. “Time adverbials like <em>just, since, for, already</em>, and <em>yet</em> are common with the present perfect tense.” They make the time reference explicit, with all its details. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>for</strong></em> shows a <strong>duration</strong> (a length of time): <em>for a long time</em>, <em>for some time</em>.</li>\n<li><em><strong>since</strong></em> shows a <strong>specific point</strong> of time: <em>since December 2021</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></li>\n<li><em><strong>yet</strong></em> and <em><strong>already</strong></em> appear mostly in <strong>questions</strong>, in conversation: <em>Has she bought the book yet? Have you eaten already?</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a> (They are common outside questions too: <em>yet</em> in negatives, <em>I haven’t eaten yet</em>; <em>already</em> in statements, <em>I have already eaten</em>.)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">For or since?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask: is it a <strong>length</strong> of time or a <strong>starting point</strong>? A length (<em>a long time, some time</em>) takes <em>for</em>. A point (<em>December 2021</em>) takes <em>since</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"present-progressive\">Present progressive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Present progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The progressive aspect in the present describes activities <strong>in progress</strong> at a given time, usually for a <strong>limited</strong> amount of time.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></p>\n<p>Structure: <strong>am / is / are</strong> + verb-<em><strong>ing</strong></em>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>am, is, are</em> are the present forms of <em>be</em> and carry the tense: <em>am</em> with <em>I</em>, <em>is</em> with a singular subject, <em>are</em> with a plural subject.</li>\n<li>The <em>-ing</em> suffix is the progressive marker.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s example: <em>I’<strong>m looking</strong> for an intern.</em> <em>I’m</em> is the short (contracted) form of <em>I am</em>. <em>Am</em> is the tense marker, used with the first person singular <em>I</em>; <em>look</em> + <em>-ing</em> is the progressive. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Tense</th>\n<th>Progressive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She <strong>is eating</strong> a mango.</em> (affirmative)</td>\n<td><em>is</em></td>\n<td><em>eating</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They <strong>are writing</strong> an exam.</em> (plural subject)</td>\n<td><em>are</em></td>\n<td><em>writing</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The car <strong>is</strong> not <strong>moving</strong> due to the traffic.</em> (negative)</td>\n<td><em>is</em></td>\n<td><em>moving</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">More in speech than in writing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: the present progressive “is more commonly used in conversations than in writing”. The lecturer adds that academic prose and other professional writing do not encourage it, and recommends the present perfect there instead. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"present-perfect-progressive\">Present perfect progressive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Present perfect progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“It is formed by adding the progressive form to the present perfect.” Here two aspects combine with each other, to add more detail to relational time. “It indicates that an action <strong>has begun in the past but continues to the present</strong>.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a> The slide gives three ways it adds detail:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>An uninterrupted action.</strong> <em>The baby <strong>has been crying</strong> for long.</em> The crying began in the past and went on until now; nobody stopped it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a> (In a positive sentence, standard English says <em>for a long time</em>; <em>for long</em> fits questions and negatives: <em>Has the baby been crying for long?</em>)</li>\n<li><strong>A recently stopped action.</strong> <em>It <strong>has been raining</strong> all day and now it is all clear.</em> The rain began in the past, went on, and stopped at the time of speaking. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>A recently picked-up habit.</strong> <em>Recently, I <strong>have been practising</strong> yoga.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the present perfect progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>has / have</strong> + <strong>been</strong> + verb-<em><strong>ing</strong></em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>has / have</em> is the auxiliary in the present: it carries the tense.</li>\n<li><em>been</em> is the V3 of <em>be</em>. <em>Has been</em> (or <em>have been</em>) together make the perfect.</li>\n<li>verb + <em>-ing</em> is the progressive marker.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence type</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Affirmative</td>\n<td><em>Amit <strong>has been driving</strong> the car from Bengaluru to Chennai.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td><em>Shirin <strong>has</strong> not <strong>been drinking</strong> water and now she is dehydrated.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plural subject</td>\n<td><em>The birds <strong>have</strong> not <strong>been responding</strong> to calls lately due to interference from artificial bird calls from the smartphones.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>With the plural subject <em>the birds</em>, <em>has</em> changes to <em>have</em>. The meaning of the form stays the same. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9lzN9jZiWiw&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a></p>\n<h2 id=\"the-future-tense\">The future tense</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 5.3 builds the future with <em><strong>will</strong></em>, then adds the same aspects as before:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What will happen? <strong>Simple future</strong>: <em>It will rain in the month of June.</em></li>\n<li>What will be finished before another future time? <strong>Future perfect</strong>: <em>She will have completed her work by then.</em></li>\n<li>What will be going on? <strong>Future progressive</strong>: <em>She will be taking the train to work from now on.</em></li>\n<li>What will have been going on for some time by a future point? <strong>Future perfect progressive</strong>: <em>I will have been living in Chennai for over ten years.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"will-and-time-adverbials\">Will and time adverbials</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Will and the future tense</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>“English employs various ways to express future time.”</li>\n<li>“The modal auxiliary verb <em>will</em> is the most often used marker of the future time reference.”</li>\n<li>“<em>Will</em> suggests a sense of <strong>prediction</strong> when used as a <strong>tense-forming auxiliary</strong>.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A modal auxiliary can have more than one meaning; here <em>will</em> is used to form the future. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a> Modal verbs, <em>will</em> and <em>shall</em> among them, are in <a href=\"#/english1/w03/modal-verbs\">Week 3</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences, each a prediction about the future: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>What is predicted</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The train <strong>will arrive</strong> in 5 minutes.</em></td>\n<td>the arrival of the train, later</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I <strong>will cook</strong> pasta in the evening.</em></td>\n<td>someone’s action later in the evening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>It <strong>will be</strong> cold in Delhi at this time of year.</em></td>\n<td>the weather, from what we know of Delhi’s climate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>What <strong>will</strong> he <strong>do</strong> if they don’t come for the meeting?</em></td>\n<td>his future reaction (a question)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Time adverbials and the future</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The future time expressions in English sometimes come along with certain <strong>time adverbials</strong> to make the time reference explicit.” They make the time clearer to the listener or reader. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a> Some of them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>tomorrow</em>;</li>\n<li><em>next</em> + a week, a day, a date, a month, a year: <em>next Monday</em>, <em>next March</em>, <em>next year</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>prepositional phrases</strong> (a preposition + a noun): <em>in the morning</em>, <em>in the afternoon</em>, <em>in the evening</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s sentences: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I will be 28 <strong>in March</strong>.</em></li>\n<li><em>I’m on the bus and I’ll reach Indore <strong>by four</strong>.</em></li>\n<li><em>They will board the plane <strong>in the morning</strong>.</em> (a prepositional phrase)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"simple-future\">Simple future</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple future</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Structure: <strong>will</strong> + <strong>V1</strong> (the bare verb). The slide writes it as “will (aux) + Verb”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I <strong>will visit</strong> the mall at the weekend.</em> (first person singular)</li>\n<li><em>It <strong>will rain</strong> in the month of June.</em> (third person singular)</li>\n<li><em>They <strong>will</strong> not <strong>come</strong> for the lunch tonight.</em> (third person plural; negative. Lunch is a midday meal, so the natural sentence is <em>They will not come for dinner tonight</em>.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The form does not change with the subject: singular or plural, it is <em>will</em> + V1. <em>Not</em> only negates the idea; it changes neither the structure nor the time reference. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">No -s after will</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>She will visit</em>, not ✗ <em>She will visits</em>: after <em>will</em>, the verb stays bare for every subject. (The lecture’s rule “will + verb”; the wrong form is shown for contrast.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"future-perfect\">Future perfect</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Future perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The perfect aspect is used to relate one time to the other. It represents <strong>relational</strong> time.” In the future perfect, the representation “is of the <strong>past time within the future</strong> or the past <strong>embedded</strong> in the future time”. The lecturer also calls the earlier event the <strong>previous future</strong>. Both events lie in the future; one comes before the other. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two future events (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>She <strong>will have done</strong> the work so the project <strong>will be completed</strong>.</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Event 2, the future time: <em>the project will be completed</em>.</li>\n<li>Event 1, the previous future: <em>she will have done the work</em>. It is embedded in Event 2: it comes before it.</li>\n<li>On a timeline both events come after now: now, then Event 1, then Event 2. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> first she does the work, then the project is completed. Both lie in the future, and the future perfect (<em>will have done</em>) marks the earlier one.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Now</th>\n<th>Previous future (Event 1)</th>\n<th>Future (Event 2)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the present</td>\n<td>she <strong>will have done</strong> the work</td>\n<td>the project <strong>will be completed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the future perfect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>will</strong> + <strong>have</strong> + <strong>V3</strong>. The slide: “aux + have (aux) + verb (participle form)”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>will</em>: the tense-forming auxiliary;</li>\n<li><em>have</em>: a second auxiliary, required in this form;</li>\n<li>the main verb in its participle form, V3. The <em>-ed</em> of <em>completed</em> is the past participle, not the simple past.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>She <strong>will have completed</strong> her work by then.</em></li>\n<li><em>She <strong>will</strong> not <strong>have completed</strong> her work.</em> (negative) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></li>\n<li><em>We <strong>will have spent</strong> most of the money by then.</em> (<em>we</em>: first person plural; <em>spent</em> is the V3 of <em>spend</em>) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The structure is the same whatever the number of the subject.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Have, never has, after will</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>After <em>will</em>, the auxiliary stays <em>have</em> for every subject: <em>She will <strong>have</strong> completed</em>, not ✗ <em>She will has completed</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"future-progressive\">Future progressive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Future progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Future progressive tense form indicates that a <strong>future action will be in progress</strong>.” Its sentences are all set in the future, but they carry slightly different meanings. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a> The slide gives three:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Timetabled future</strong>: <em>They <strong>will be having</strong> lunch around 12:00 p.m. so don’t call them now.</em> It sounds like someone’s timetable. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Future routine</strong>: <em>I’<strong>ll be travelling</strong> by bus until the bike is fixed.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>Future habit</strong>: <em>I’<strong>ll be taking</strong> my day off on Saturdays.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>I’ll</em> is the contracted form of <em>I will</em>: the apostrophe and <em>ll</em> stand for <em>will</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the future progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>will</strong> + <strong>be</strong> + verb-<em><strong>ing</strong></em>. The slide: “aux + be + verb (progressive form -ing)”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>She <strong>will be taking</strong> the train to work from now on.</em></li>\n<li><em>She <strong>will</strong> not <strong>be taking</strong> the train to work.</em> (negative)</li>\n<li><em><strong>Will</strong> she <strong>be taking</strong> the train to work?</em> (question)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In the question, the auxiliary <em>will</em> moves before the subject. This is called <strong>subject–auxiliary inversion</strong>. Nothing else in the structure changes. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"future-perfect-progressive\">Future perfect progressive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Future perfect progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The future perfect progressive is formed by the combination of <strong>future perfect</strong> with the <strong>progressive</strong> aspect.” “In terms of time, it refers to the <strong>previous future within the future</strong>.” The progressive adds that this action has been <strong>in progress</strong> at that point of time. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a> It can give four meanings:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A previous future state will remain a current future state</strong>: <em>I <strong>will have been living</strong> in Chennai for over ten years.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>A future ongoing action will still be occurring</strong>: <em>I <strong>will have been performing</strong> in the theatre group for over a year.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></li>\n<li><strong>A future series of events will go on</strong>: <em>She <strong>will have been visiting</strong> him for years by then.</em> The visits will go on; nothing comes to an end. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></li>\n<li><strong>A future long-lasting action will continue</strong>: <em>I <strong>will have been sitting</strong> here for all time of the day.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a> (Standard English: <em>all day</em>, or <em>the whole day</em>.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A debated form</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer calls this form “a bit contentious”: some people say it is not a real tense form. It is true that not all verbs give a meaningful context in it; only certain kinds of verbs go well with it. But it is possible, and it adds detail to future time. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Structure of the future perfect progressive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>will</strong> + <strong>have</strong> + <strong>been</strong> + verb-<em><strong>ing</strong></em>. The slide: “aux + have (aux) + be (participle form) + verb (progressive form -ing)”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>will</em>: the tense auxiliary;</li>\n<li><em>have</em>: an auxiliary;</li>\n<li><em>been</em>: the V3 (participle form) of <em>be</em>;</li>\n<li>verb + <em>-ing</em>: the progressive marker.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>She <strong>will have been performing</strong> in the group for years by then.</em></li>\n<li><em>She <strong>will</strong> not <strong>have been working</strong> there for so long.</em> (negative) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a></li>\n<li><em><strong>Will</strong> they <strong>have been doing</strong> that work?</em> (question: <em>will</em> moves before the subject) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-twelve-forms-together\">The twelve forms together</h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>The lectures’ example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple past</td>\n<td>V2; <em>did</em> + V1 in negatives and questions</td>\n<td><em>returned</em>; <em>did not finish</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect</td>\n<td><em>had</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>had returned</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past progressive</td>\n<td><em>was / were</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>was walking</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>had been</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>had been knitting</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple present</td>\n<td>V1 or V1 + <em>-s</em>; <em>do / does</em> + V1 in negatives and questions</td>\n<td><em>drinks</em>; <em>do not want</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect</td>\n<td><em>has / have</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>has arrived</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present progressive</td>\n<td><em>am / is / are</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>is eating</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>has / have been</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>has been driving</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple future</td>\n<td><em>will</em> + V1</td>\n<td><em>will visit</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect</td>\n<td><em>will have</em> + V3</td>\n<td><em>will have completed</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future progressive</td>\n<td><em>will be</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>will be taking</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect progressive</td>\n<td><em>will have been</em> + V-<em>ing</em></td>\n<td><em>will have been performing</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Read the verb group from the left</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>first</strong> word carries the tense: <em>did, had, was, were</em> → past; <em>do, does, has, have, am, is, are</em> → present; <em>will</em> → future. (In the simple past affirmative, the verb itself carries it: <em>returned, drove</em>. In the simple present affirmative, nothing visible does.)</li>\n<li>Then look for the aspect: a <strong>V3</strong> after <em>have / has / had</em> → perfect; <em><strong>-ing</strong></em> after a form of <em>be</em> → progressive; <em><strong>been</strong></em> + <em>-ing</em> → perfect progressive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For example, <em>had | been | writing</em>: <em>had</em> = past; <em>had been</em> = perfect; <em>writing</em> = progressive. So it is the past perfect progressive.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Same form, different meanings</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture ends with a caution: one form can carry different meanings, as the future progressive (three meanings) and the future perfect progressive (four) show. Be careful about the form you use and the meaning you intend. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qTJ9gMjcb1c&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"subject-verb-agreement\">Subject–verb agreement</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>Raju likes pizza</em>, the small <em>-s</em> on <em>likes</em> repeats what <em>Raju</em> already tells us: one person, neither the speaker nor the listener. The verb carries a <strong>copy</strong> of the subject’s features. <strong>Agreement</strong> means that this copy must match.</p>\n<p>Why it matters: language learning has two parts, <strong>accuracy</strong> and <strong>appropriateness</strong>. Together they bring confidence in speaking and make writing impressive. Agreement is directly connected with accuracy: it is “the key to grammaticality”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentence, subject and predicate</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A sentence has two parts: the <strong>subject</strong> and the <strong>predicate</strong>. Agreement works between the subject and the <strong>verb</strong> inside the predicate. The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=165s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:45</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sentence</strong> = [Subject + Predicate].</li>\n<li><strong>Predicate</strong> = [Verb + Object(s) + adjuncts/adverbs]: everything except the subject. <strong>Adjuncts</strong> are extra parts that add information, such as adverbs.</li>\n<li><strong>Complex sentence</strong> = Subject + Predicate, where the predicate contains an <strong>embedded sentence</strong> as an object. For example (ours): <em>Ramu knows [that Raju likes pizza]</em>: the sentence in brackets is the object of <em>knows</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"how-agreement-works\">How agreement works</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Agreement</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A noun in the subject position has <strong>features</strong>: <strong>person</strong>, <strong>number</strong> and <strong>gender</strong>. In subject–verb agreement, these features of the subject must <strong>also show up on the verb</strong>. If they match, the sentence is grammatical; if they do not, it is ungrammatical. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n<p>In English, <strong>gender does not take part</strong> in agreement. Only <strong>person</strong> and <strong>number</strong> matter. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Raju likes pizza (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Find the subject: <em>Raju</em>.</li>\n<li>Its features: <strong>third person</strong> (a proper name, neither the speaker nor the listener) and <strong>singular</strong> (one person). It is also masculine, but gender plays no part in English. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></li>\n<li>These features show up on the verb as the small ending <em>-s</em>: <em>Raju <strong>likes</strong> pizza</em>.</li>\n<li>Without it, ✗ <em>Raju like pizza</em> is ungrammatical: the subject’s features are missing on the verb. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>likes</em>, because the subject is third person singular.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Raju and Ramu like pizza (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The subject is <em>Raju and Ramu</em>: two people, so <strong>plural</strong>, and still <strong>third person</strong>.</li>\n<li>For a plural subject in the present tense, nothing appears on the verb. The marker is <strong>zero</strong>: the lecturer writes <em>like∅</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></li>\n<li>So: <em>Raju and Ramu <strong>like</strong> pizza</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>like</em>, with a zero marker.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">A verb has no number of its own</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Subject–verb agreement” sounds like two partners in a negotiation. It is not. Verbs are not singular or plural by themselves: they <strong>take</strong> the features of the subject. “Verbs host agreement features for matching.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>. The lecturer keeps every sentence in the present tense, so that the patterns are easy to see. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Features of the subject</th>\n<th>On the verb</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Raju <strong>likes</strong> pizza.</em></td>\n<td>third person, singular</td>\n<td><em>-s</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>I <strong>go</strong> to the office on time.</em></td>\n<td>first person singular</td>\n<td>zero, like a plural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>You <strong>go</strong> to the market every week.</em></td>\n<td>second person: plural in structure</td>\n<td>zero</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She <strong>leaves</strong> her office late in the evening.</em></td>\n<td>third person, singular, feminine</td>\n<td><em>-s</em>; nothing for gender</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>We <strong>are</strong> happy.</em></td>\n<td>first person, plural</td>\n<td><em>are</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>The school <strong>is</strong> closed.</em></td>\n<td>third person, singular</td>\n<td><em>is</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">I and you are special</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><em><strong>I</strong></em>: the first person keeps special characteristics across languages. <em>I</em> is one person, yet it follows the <strong>plural</strong> pattern: <em>I go to the office on time</em>, not ✗ <em>I goes to the office on time</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></li>\n<li><em><strong>you</strong></em>: the second person is always taken as <strong>plural</strong> in English, even when it means one person: <em>you go</em>, <em>you are</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></li>\n<li><em><strong>am</strong></em>: “<em>Am</em> is a special case tense marker”: it comes <strong>only</strong> with the first person <em>I</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">I follows the plural pattern only in the present</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In the past, <em>I</em> takes <em>was</em>, like a singular subject: <em>I was reading</em>, not ✗ <em>I were reading</em>. (This line is added here; the lecture keeps all its sentences in the present.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Use your own language</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer: check the language you speak. The first person works differently there too. Another pattern is found in every language: in an <strong>imperative</strong> sentence (a command), the subject <em>you</em> is dropped: <em>Sit down</em>. Noticing these likenesses and differences makes English agreement easier to understand. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The method: find the subject first</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Things to notice” at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1965s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:45</a>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Feature on the subject must show up on the verb.”</li>\n<li>“Verbs host agreement features for matching.”</li>\n<li>“Features on the subject when not obvious becomes a cognitive exercise.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the work has three steps: <strong>decide what the subject is</strong>, <strong>work out its features</strong> (person and number), then <strong>choose the marker</strong> on the verb. Once the subject is clear, the rest is simple. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"nouns-that-need-a-second-look\">Nouns that need a second look</h3>\n<p>The lecturer asks us not to treat the cases below as exceptions. Agreement has no exceptions; these sentences only need careful thought about the subject. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Same form for one and many: sheep, deer</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some nouns do not change their form between singular and plural. From the noun alone we cannot tell one from many; the verb shows which we mean. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>The sheep <strong>is</strong> a harmless creature.</em> (one sheep)</li>\n<li><em>The sheep <strong>are</strong> expensive for some farmers.</em> (many sheep)</li>\n<li><em>Deer <strong>is</strong> not available everywhere.</em> (one) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a> (For one animal, standard English adds an article, as with <em>the sheep</em>: <em>The deer <strong>is</strong> drinking water</em>, my example.)</li>\n<li><em>Deer <strong>are</strong> free on IIT Madras campus.</em> (many: “a bunch of them”)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">No plural form: mathematics, politics, measles</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some nouns end in <em>-s</em> but are <strong>singular</strong>: the <em>-s</em> is not a plural marker at all. Examples are <em>mathematics, politics</em>, and the names of some diseases, like <em>measles</em>. Such a noun takes singular agreement. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Mathematics <strong>is</strong> tough to follow.</em> <em>Mathematics <strong>gives</strong> nightmares to some people.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></li>\n<li><em>Politics <strong>is</strong> easy for everyone to understand.</em> <em>Politics <strong>requires</strong> careful speech.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">No singular form: glasses, jeans, police</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some nouns are used only as plurals. They always have plural features, so they always need plural features on the verb. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>My glasses <strong>are</strong> blue.</em> (for spectacles we never say ✗ <em>My glass is blue</em>)</li>\n<li><em>Their jeans <strong>leave</strong> colour.</em> (<em>leave</em>, no <em>-s</em>: plural)</li>\n<li><em>Police <strong>are</strong> vigilant during festivals.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Indefinites are singular</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Pronouns that do not refer to anything definite are called <strong>indefinites</strong>. They always take a <strong>singular</strong> verb. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a> They come in pairs that mean the same thing (<em>everyone</em> or <em>everybody</em>), and they split between people (animate) and things (inanimate): <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>For people</th>\n<th>For things</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>everyone / everybody</td>\n<td>everything</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>someone / somebody</td>\n<td>something</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>no one / nobody</td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Everyone <strong>comes</strong> to the football match.</em> <em>Someone <strong>is</strong> speaking.</em> <em>Nobody <strong>comes</strong> after 5 pm.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></li>\n<li><em>Everything <strong>is</strong> apparent.</em> <em>Something <strong>is</strong> missing.</em> <em>Nothing <strong>gets</strong> attention.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>We cannot say ✗ <em>Everything are apparent</em>: <em>everything</em> does not refer to anything particular, so it is taken as singular. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A quantity of time is singular</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A quantity of time (<em>4 hours, 5 minutes, 5 years</em>) is taken <strong>as a whole</strong>, so it takes a singular verb, even though the noun is plural. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Five years <strong>is</strong> not a long time in history.</em></li>\n<li><em>Sometimes 5 minutes <strong>appears</strong> too long.</em></li>\n<li><em>Four hours <strong>is</strong> fine.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Notice them when you read</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Read one page of any book and you will find one or two examples of these. Watch for them when you read, speak and listen to others. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"when-the-verb-agrees-with-another-noun\">When the verb agrees with another noun</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Agreement with plural complements</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Here a <strong>complement</strong> is the noun phrase that comes <strong>after</strong> the verb <em>be</em> and completes the sentence (<em>the children in the park</em>). In some sentences, the agreement on the verb comes from this complement. This is tricky; the lecturer asks us only to pay special attention to such sentences. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>What distracts me <strong>are</strong> the children in the park.</em> (<em>are</em> comes from <em>children</em>) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a></li>\n<li><em>The most annoying thing last week <strong>were</strong> buses on the road.</em> (<em>were</em> comes from <em>buses</em>)</li>\n<li><em>The most beautiful thing that I saw last week <strong>was</strong> the painting in the museum.</em> (<em>was</em> comes from <em>the painting</em>) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>In careful writing, a subject with a clear singular head keeps a singular verb: <em>The most annoying thing last week <strong>was</strong> the buses on the road.</em> The plural verb is common after <em>what</em>, which has no number of its own: <em>What distracts me <strong>are</strong> the children in the park</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">There as an empty subject</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>There <strong>are</strong> several monkeys on the tree.</em></p>\n<p><em>There</em> is an <strong>expletive</strong> subject: an empty word that only fills the subject position. The verb <em>are</em> agrees with <em>several monkeys</em>, which come after it. At some level of processing in the mind, <em>monkeys</em> is taken as the subject of this sentence. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Either … or, neither … nor: the closest noun</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>either … or</em> and <em>neither … nor</em> sentences, the <strong>second</strong> noun, the one after <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, takes part in agreement. (<em>Either … or</em> is the affirmative pair and <em>neither … nor</em> the negative one; they work the same way.) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Noun after <em>or / nor</em></th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Either my father or my siblings <strong>are</strong> with me at home.</em></td>\n<td><em>my siblings</em>: plural</td>\n<td><em>are</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Either my siblings or my father <strong>is</strong> with me at home.</em></td>\n<td><em>my father</em>: singular</td>\n<td><em>is</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Neither my father nor my siblings <strong>are</strong> with me at home.</em></td>\n<td><em>my siblings</em>: plural</td>\n<td><em>are</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Neither my siblings nor my father <strong>is</strong> with me at home.</em></td>\n<td><em>my father</em>: singular</td>\n<td><em>is</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Put the plural last</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both orders are grammatical, but “English tends to put plurals at the last”. So the usual way to say it is <em>Either my father or my siblings are with me at home</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pv-eV3nVceE&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Small changes to slide sentences</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A few slide sentences leave out an article or a plural ending. This page adds the missing word; the verb, which is the point, is the same. The slides have: <em>He reads newspaper in the morning</em> (lecture 5.2), <em>The sheep is harmless creature</em>, <em>Mathematics gives nightmare to some people</em>, <em>Police are vigilant during festival</em>, <em>Five years is not long time in history</em>, <em>Most annoying thing last week were buses on the road</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"agreement-practice\">Agreement practice</h2>\n<p>Lecture 5.5 practises agreement with 25 sentences. Each slide gives a sentence with a choice (<em>is</em> or <em>are</em>), the right answer, and the reason. The steps are always the same: <strong>find the subject</strong>, decide whether it is <strong>singular or plural</strong>, then <strong>choose the verb</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"and-or-nor\">And, or, nor</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">And makes a plural</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“When the subject of a sentence is composed of two or more nouns or pronouns connected by <em><strong>and</strong></em>, use a <strong>plural</strong> verb.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Annie and her brothers <strong>are</strong> at school.</em> (<em>Annie</em> and <em>her brothers</em> are joined by the conjunction <em>and</em>.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Singulars joined by or / nor stay singular</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“When two or more <strong>singular</strong> nouns or pronouns are connected by <em><strong>or</strong></em> or <em><strong>nor</strong></em>, use a <strong>singular</strong> verb.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><em>The dog or the cat <strong>is</strong> outside.</em> (<em>the dog</em>, not <em>the dogs</em>; <em>the cat</em>, not <em>the cats</em>: both singular)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Singular and plural joined by or / nor: the nearer part decides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“When a <strong>compound subject</strong> contains both a singular and a plural noun or pronoun joined by <em>or</em> or <em>nor</em>, the verb should agree with the part of the subject that is <strong>nearer the verb</strong>.” A compound subject is a subject made of two or more nouns. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></p>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li><em>The dog or the cats <strong>are</strong> outside.</em> (nearer the verb: <em>the cats</em>, plural)</li>\n<li><em>Either my shoes or your coat <strong>is</strong> always on the floor.</em> (nearer the verb: <em>your coat</em>, singular) <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two lectures, one rule</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 5.4 says “the noun after <em>or / nor</em>”; lecture 5.5 says “the part nearer the verb”. In these sentences they are the same noun: the second noun stands just before the verb.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"doesn-t-and-don-t\">Doesn’t and don’t</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Doesn’t and don’t</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“<em>Doesn’t</em> is a contraction of <em>does not</em> and should be used only with a <strong>singular</strong> subject. <em>Don’t</em> is a contraction of <em>do not</em> and should be used only with a <strong>plural</strong> subject. The exception to this rule appears in the case of the first person and second person pronouns <em><strong>I</strong></em> and <em><strong>you</strong></em>. With these pronouns, the contraction <em>don’t</em> should be used.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<p>A <strong>contraction</strong> is a short form made by joining two words with an apostrophe: <em>does not → doesn’t</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Subject</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>5. <em>George and Tara <strong>don’t</strong> want to see that movie.</em></td>\n<td>plural (two people)</td>\n<td>don’t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. <em>Vaiga <strong>doesn’t</strong> know the answer.</em></td>\n<td>singular</td>\n<td>doesn’t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7. <em>He <strong>doesn’t</strong> know.</em></td>\n<td>singular</td>\n<td>doesn’t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8. <em>They <strong>don’t</strong> know.</em></td>\n<td>plural</td>\n<td>don’t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9. <em>I <strong>don’t</strong> know.</em></td>\n<td><em>I</em>: the exception</td>\n<td>don’t</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10. <em>You <strong>don’t</strong> tell me that!</em></td>\n<td><em>you</em>: the exception</td>\n<td>don’t</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<p>So never ✗ <em>I doesn’t know</em> or ✗ <em>You doesn’t tell me that</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a> This “exception” is the pattern of lecture 5.4: <em>I</em> and <em>you</em> follow the plural.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-phrase-between-subject-and-verb\">A phrase between subject and verb</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The phrase does not change the subject</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Do not be misled by a phrase that comes between the subject and the verb. The verb agrees with the <strong>subject</strong>, not with a noun or pronoun in the phrase.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a> · <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>The real subject</th>\n<th>Verb</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>11. <em>One of the boxes <strong>is</strong> open.</em></td>\n<td><em>one</em></td>\n<td>is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12. <em>The people who listen to that music <strong>are</strong> few.</em></td>\n<td><em>the people</em>: plural</td>\n<td>are</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13. <em>The team captain, as well as his players, <strong>is</strong> anxious.</em></td>\n<td><em>the team captain</em></td>\n<td>is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14. <em>The book, including all the chapters in the first section, <strong>is</strong> boring.</em></td>\n<td><em>the book</em></td>\n<td>is</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15. <em>The woman with all the dogs <strong>walks</strong> down my street.</em></td>\n<td><em>the woman</em></td>\n<td>walks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One of the boxes (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Find the subject. It is not all of the boxes; it is <strong>one</strong> of them. <em>Of the boxes</em> is a phrase that only says which group the one comes from.</li>\n<li><em>One</em> is singular.</li>\n<li>So the verb is singular: <em>One of the boxes <strong>is</strong> open</em>, not ✗ <em>One of the boxes are open</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>is</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Bracket the phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To find the real subject, put the phrase between the subject and the verb in brackets, the way the lecturer boxes it on the slide, and read the sentence without it: <em>The team captain [as well as his players] <strong>is</strong> anxious</em>; <em>The woman [with all the dogs] <strong>walks</strong> down my street</em>. What is left must agree.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"singular-words-and-plural-words\">Singular words and plural words</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Each, either, everyone … are singular</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“The words <em>each, each one, either, neither, everyone, everybody, anybody, anyone, nobody, somebody, someone</em>, and <em>no one</em> are singular and require a singular verb.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></p>\n<ol start=\"16\">\n<li><em>Each of these donuts <strong>is</strong> yummy.</em></li>\n<li><em>Everybody <strong>knows</strong> Mr. Ram.</em> (not ✗ <em>Everybody know Mr. Ram</em>)</li>\n<li><em>Either <strong>is</strong> correct.</em> (not ✗ <em>Either are correct</em>)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Civics, mathematics, measles, news are singular</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Nouns such as <em>civics, mathematics, dollars, measles</em>, and <em>news</em> require singular verbs.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n<ol start=\"19\">\n<li><em>The news <strong>is</strong> on at six.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><em><strong>Dollars</strong></em> is a special case: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>talking about an <strong>amount of money</strong> → singular: <em>Five dollars <strong>is</strong> a lot of money.</em></li>\n<li>referring to the <strong>dollars themselves</strong> (the currency) → plural: <em>Dollars <strong>are</strong> often used instead of rubles in Russia.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Scissors, tweezers, trousers are plural</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Nouns such as <em>scissors, tweezers, trousers</em> etc. require plural verbs. (There are two parts to these things.)” We say <em>a pair of scissors</em>, <em>a pair of trousers</em>: one item, seen as a pair. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n<ol start=\"20\">\n<li><em>These scissors <strong>are</strong> dull.</em></li>\n<li><em>Those trousers <strong>are</strong> made of wool.</em></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Collective nouns are singular</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Collective nouns are words that imply more than one person but that are considered singular and take a singular verb, such as <em>group, team, committee, class</em>, and <em>family</em>.” <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ALjHStRxE7M&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a> (That is the rule when the group acts as one unit, as in all the sentences below. When the members act separately, British English and many grammar books use a plural verb: <em>The jury were divided in their opinions</em>.)</p>\n<ol start=\"22\">\n<li><em>The team <strong>runs</strong> during practice.</em></li>\n<li><em>The committee <strong>decides</strong> how to proceed.</em></li>\n<li><em>The family <strong>has</strong> a long history.</em></li>\n<li><em>That class <strong>is</strong> very noisy.</em></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Police is not like team</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Team, committee, family, class, group</em> are collective nouns: they take a <strong>singular</strong> verb (lecture 5.5). <em>Police</em> is different: lecture 5.4 puts it with the nouns that have <strong>no singular form</strong>, so it takes a <strong>plural</strong> verb: <em>Police <strong>are</strong> vigilant during festivals</em>. Learn the two lists separately.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two-part things are plural</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Glasses, jeans</em> (lecture 5.4) and <em>scissors, tweezers, trousers</em> (lecture 5.5) all have two parts, and all take a plural verb.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tense</strong> places an action in time (past, present, future), through changes around the verb. <strong>Aspect</strong> tells how the action sits in that time: simple (absolute time), perfect (one time embedded in another: relational), progressive (in progress), perfect progressive (an embedded action in progress).</li>\n<li><strong>The tense carrier</strong> is one part only: the V2 in the simple past affirmative, or the first auxiliary (<em>did, had, was, were</em>; <em>do, does, has, have, am, is, are</em>; <em>will</em>). V3 and <em>-ing</em> never carry tense. The simple present affirmative has no visible tense marker; its <em>-s</em> is an agreement marker.</li>\n<li><strong>Past</strong>: simple (V2; <em>did</em> + V1): past and finished, also story narration · perfect (<em>had</em> + V3): past before past · progressive (<em>was/were</em> + V-<em>ing</em>): ongoing in the past · perfect progressive (<em>had been</em> + V-<em>ing</em>): an embedded past action in progress.</li>\n<li><strong>Present</strong>: simple (V1 or V1 + <em>-s</em>; <em>do/does</em> + V1): a state, a habit, an action now · perfect (<em>has/have</em> + V3): past with present relevance (still going, a change now, a state that continues); <em>for</em> = duration, <em>since</em> = point of time, <em>just, already, yet</em> · progressive (<em>am/is/are</em> + V-<em>ing</em>): in progress now, for a limited time; more in speech · perfect progressive (<em>has/have been</em> + V-<em>ing</em>): began in the past and continues (uninterrupted, recently stopped, a new habit).</li>\n<li><strong>Future</strong>: <em>will</em>, a modal, suggests prediction; time adverbials make the time explicit · simple (<em>will</em> + V1) · perfect (<em>will have</em> + V3): past within the future · progressive (<em>will be</em> + V-<em>ing</em>): timetabled future, routine, habit · perfect progressive (<em>will have been</em> + V-<em>ing</em>): previous future in progress; debated, not for every verb.</li>\n<li>Mental-process verbs (<em>enjoy, appreciate, love, understand</em>) usually stay simple; this holds best for state verbs (<em>understand, know</em>), while <em>I am enjoying the party</em> is correct.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreement</strong>: the subject’s person and number show up on the verb; gender takes no part. Third person singular → <em>-s, is, has, does, doesn’t</em>. Plural subjects and <em>you</em> → zero, <em>are, have, do, don’t</em>. <em>I</em> → zero, <em>have, do, don’t</em>, and its own <em>am</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the subject</strong>: <em>and</em> → plural · <em>or / nor</em> → the noun nearer the verb (English puts the plural last) · a phrase in between changes nothing (<em>one of the boxes is</em>) · <em>there are several monkeys</em> (the verb agrees with the noun after it) · plural complements (<em>what distracts me are the children</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Always singular</strong>: indefinites (<em>each, either, neither, everyone, somebody, nobody, everything …</em>); <em>civics, mathematics, politics, measles, news</em>; an amount (<em>five years, five dollars</em>); collective nouns (<em>group, team, committee, class, family</em>). <strong>Always plural</strong>: <em>glasses, jeans, police, scissors, tweezers, trousers</em>; <em>dollars</em> as a currency. <strong>Same form</strong>: <em>sheep, deer</em>, so the verb shows the number.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name each form and the word that carries the tense. (a) <em>She had been knitting the quilt for the baby before the family arrived for Christmas.</em> (b) <em>The birds have not been responding to calls lately.</em> (c) <em>We will have spent most of the money by then.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Read each verb group from the left.</p>\n<p>(a) <em>had | been | knitting</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>had</em> carries the tense: past.</li>\n<li><em>had been</em> = <em>had</em> + the V3 of <em>be</em>: perfect.</li>\n<li><em>knitting</em> = <em>knit</em> + <em>-ing</em>: progressive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Past perfect progressive.</strong> The knitting (Event 1) was in progress before the family arrived (Event 2).</p>\n<p>(b) <em>have | been | responding</em> (<em>not</em> is only the negation marker)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>have</em> carries the tense: present (<em>have</em>, not <em>has</em>, because <em>the birds</em> is plural).</li>\n<li><em>have been</em>: perfect.</li>\n<li><em>responding</em>: progressive.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Present perfect progressive.</strong></p>\n<p>(c) <em>will | have | spent</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>will</em> carries the tense: future.</li>\n<li><em>have</em> + V3 (<em>spent</em>, from <em>spend</em>): perfect.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Future perfect.</strong> By that future time, the spending will already lie in its past.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>They had reached home before the parcel arrived.</em> Which happened first, and how does the sentence show it?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>There are two events: <em>had reached</em> and <em>arrived</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Had reached</em> is the past perfect (<em>had</em> + V3). It marks the previous past: Event 1.</li>\n<li><em>Arrived</em> is the simple past. It marks the later past: Event 2.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> they reached home first; then the parcel arrived. Event 1 is embedded in Event 2.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">(a) In <em>She drinks tea in the evening</em>, what does the <em>-s</em> of <em>drinks</em> mark? (b) <em>For</em> or <em>since</em>? <em>I have not gone on a holiday ___ a long time.</em> <em>She has not come home ___ December 2021.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a) <strong>Agreement, not tense.</strong> The <em>-s</em> shows that the verb agrees with the third person singular subject <em>she</em>. With a plural subject it disappears (<em>They drink tea in the evening</em>), and the sentence is still in the present. The simple present affirmative has no visible tense marker. In the negative, the tense shows on <em>do</em> or <em>does</em>: <em>She does not make flower bouquets.</em></p>\n<p>(b) <strong>for</strong> a long time: a duration, a length of time. <strong>since</strong> December 2021: a specific point of time.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which meaning of the future progressive does each sentence show? (a) <em>They will be having lunch around 12:00 p.m. so don’t call them now.</em> (b) <em>I’ll be travelling by bus until the bike is fixed.</em> (c) <em>I’ll be taking my day off on Saturdays.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>All three are <em>will</em> + <em>be</em> + verb-<em>ing</em>, but the meanings differ:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Timetabled future</strong>: lunch at a fixed time, as on a timetable.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Future routine</strong>: how I will travel regularly until something changes.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Future habit</strong>: what I will do every Saturday.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The form is the same; the meaning is different, as lecture 5.3 warns.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>Is</em> or <em>are</em>? (a) <em>Either my shoes or your coat ___ always on the floor.</em> (b) <em>The team captain, as well as his players, ___ anxious.</em> (c) <em>Five dollars ___ a lot of money.</em> (d) <em>Police ___ vigilant during festivals.</em> (e) <em>Everything ___ apparent.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>is</strong>. A plural (<em>my shoes</em>) and a singular (<em>your coat</em>) are joined by <em>or</em>, so the part nearer the verb decides: <em>your coat</em>, singular.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>is</strong>. The subject is <em>the team captain</em>; <em>as well as his players</em> is a phrase in between and does not change the subject.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>is</strong>. An amount of money is taken as a whole, so it is singular.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>are</strong>. <em>Police</em> has no singular form; it always takes a plural verb.</li>\n<li>(e) <strong>is</strong>. <em>Everything</em> is an indefinite; indefinites are singular.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct each sentence. (a) ✗ <em>Raju like pizza.</em> (b) ✗ <em>She did not finished her work on time.</em> (c) ✗ <em>I doesn’t know.</em> (d) ✗ <em>One of the boxes are open.</em> (e) ✗ <em>She will has completed her work by then.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <em>Raju <strong>likes</strong> pizza.</em> The subject is third person singular, so its features show on the verb as <em>-s</em>.</li>\n<li>(b) <em>She did not <strong>finish</strong> her work on time.</em> <em>Did</em> carries the past tense; the main verb stays in its bare form.</li>\n<li>(c) <em>I <strong>don’t</strong> know.</em> <em>I</em> and <em>you</em> always take <em>don’t</em>.</li>\n<li>(d) <em>One of the boxes <strong>is</strong> open.</em> The subject is <em>one</em>; <em>of the boxes</em> is a phrase in between.</li>\n<li>(e) <em>She will <strong>have</strong> completed her work by then.</em> The future perfect is <em>will</em> + <em>have</em> + V3, and <em>have</em> never changes to <em>has</em> after <em>will</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n"});
